# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# MOASYS-Vault — Music ignore list (EXAMPLE)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# A "do not warn me about this" list for your music library. Anything listed
# here is silently dropped from output/<drive>/music/warnings.json on every
# run, and counted in the run summary.
#
# Ignore lists are per DRIVE as well as per type, because warning paths are
# relative to that drive's root_path. `ignored/server/music.yaml` only affects
# runs against the root named "Server".
#
# How to use this file:
#   1. Copy it to `ignored/<drive>/music.yaml` (drop the `.example` suffix).
#      The real file is gitignored so your personal decisions stay out of
#      version control.
#   2. Add names under the level they belong to.
#   3. Re-run `npm run music <drive>`. Matching warnings disappear.
#
# ── Levels ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
#   folders   a category folder      - Other Music
#   artists   one artist folder      - Pink Floyd
#   albums    one album              - The Wall   (or  Pink Floyd/The Wall)
#   songs     one track, qualified   - The Wall/01 - In the Flesh.flac
#
# Names are matched at their own level and are CATEGORY-INDEPENDENT: an album
# listed here is silenced whether it sits in Music/, Other Music/, or both.
# That is also what lets an entry reach warn_duplicate_album, which reports
# `Artist/Album` with no category to anchor to.
#
# ── Qualifiers ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `songs:` entries must name their album — a bare `01 - Intro.flac` would
# match in every album in the library. `albums:` may be bare or qualified with
# the artist; qualify it whenever the album name isn't distinctive (all the
# soundtracks under a shared `Soundtrack` artist folder, for instance). A `/`
# means "somewhere above", not "immediately above".
#
# ── Scope ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# An entry silences EVERY warning at or below its level — tag mismatches,
# missing tags, track gaps, quality inconsistency, all of them. There is no
# per-warning-type scoping. To turn off a whole warning type instead, set
# `checks.warn_*: false` in rules/music.yaml.
#
# ── Matching ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Exact per level (not a prefix), case-insensitive, and either slash works.
#
# ── Finding names ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Take them from the `path` values in output/<drive>/music/warnings.json. Most
# music warnings use `<Category>/<Artist>/<Album>`, so drop the leading
# category and keep the last one or two segments.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────


# Silence everything under a category folder
# folders:
#   - Other Music
#   - Other Soundtracks


# Silence a whole artist
# (Useful when an artist's catalog has a known issue you can't fix — a
# folder/tag mismatch set by the original publisher, say.)
# artists:
#   - Some Artist
#   - Various Artists


# Silence one album
# (Drops every warning on it: compilation_detected, folder_tag_mismatch,
# missing_tags, track_number_mismatch, quality_inconsistent, mono_audio.
# If you only want to drop one check across the whole library, use
# `checks.warn_*: false` in rules/music.yaml instead.)
# albums:
#   - Pink Floyd/The Wall
#   - 2Pac/All Eyez on Me
#   # Bare is fine when the album name is distinctive on its own:
#   - Kid A


# Silence one track
# songs:
#   - The Wall/01 - In the Flesh.flac
#   - Pink Floyd/The Wall/01 - In the Flesh.flac
